 ## Overview
-Block Predicate Filtering is a culling technique that evaluates a user-defined predicate on each grid block using affine arithmetic to determine if the block's domain can possibly contain a zero of the scalar field, skipping blocks that cannot before scalar evaluation [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Resumable Ray Marching Iterator](1505-resumable-ray-marching-iterator.md) by providing a coarse-grained filtering mechanism that reduces the number of expensive scalar field evaluations required during ray marching. Compared to naive block mask extraction, it filters blocks before scalar evaluation, reducing computation [S1, unofficial].
+Block Predicate Filtering is a culling technique that evaluates a user-defined predicate on each grid block using affine arithmetic to determine if the block's domain can possibly contain a zero of the scalar field, skipping blocks that cannot before scalar evaluation [S1, unofficial]. Compared to naive block mask extraction, it filters blocks before scalar evaluation, reducing computation [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Resumable Ray Marching Iterator](1505-resumable-ray-marching-iterator.md) by providing a coarse-grained filtering mechanism that reduces the number of expensive scalar field evaluations required during ray marching [G].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates on a grid decomposed into blocks (macrocells). For each block, it determines whether the scalar field defined over that block's spatial domain can possibly take the value zero. If the predicate returns `false`, the block is culled entirely, avoiding the cost of evaluating the scalar field and its derivatives within that block.
